Description: sparc lab of wsh-pve: unattended guest installs into Proxmox VE VMs
 The lab of the wsh-pve project drives a guest's serial console and framebuffer
 the way a person would and records every step. Its main command installs a
 named target (Solaris 2.6 on a SPARCstation 5 or 20, MS-DOS 6.22 or FreeDOS
 1.4 from floppies) into a new VM on
 a Proxmox VE node, from install media the user provides, and leaves a powered
 off VM with known, publicly documented login details.
 .
 wsh-lab wisp-vm puts wisp, the project's tiny Linux test system, or Freedoom
 in fbDOOM into a new VM from the project's published, SHA-256 checked files
 (squashfs-tools builds the game's data disk).
 .
 Commands: wsh-lab (install, targets, media, wisp-vm, run, serve, ...),
 wsh-sun-proms and wsh-lab-mcp, an MCP server that lets an AI assistant see and
 type on an allowed VM. The MCP server's Python SDK is not packaged in Debian:
 wsh-lab-mcp --setup installs it into a venv under /var/lib/wsh-lab from the
 pinned, hash-checked requirements in this package.
 .
 wsh-sun-proms installs Sun boot PROM images you are entitled to use, from a
 source you provide (a directory, an archive, a git repository with your own
 credentials, or an HTTPS URL), into /usr/share/kvm, where QEMU loads firmware
 from. Each file is checked against a manifest of known images (name, size,
 SHA-256); no images come with the package.
 .
 The package works with whatever qemu-server is installed and depends on none
 of the wsh-pve packages. tesseract-ocr (recommended) is needed for targets
 driven on a framebuffer or a graphical screen, such as ReactOS's setup;
 vpx-tools for the framebuffer videos of a run.
